Revision history for the tg skill

0.01  2026-09-07
      - Initial scaffold (TGT-001): lib/, cli/, t/, tickets/, README,
        LICENSE, .env.
      - D2TG::Config (TGT-002): reads D2TG_TOKEN/D2TG_CHAT_ID, startup
        guard warns to STDERR and refuses when D2TG_CHAT_ID is unset.
      - cli/poller (TGT-003): d2 tg.poller entrypoint calling the
        startup guard; poll loop itself not yet implemented.
      - D2TG::Telegram (TGT-004): minimal Bot API client (get_me,
        get_updates, get_file) over HTTP::Tiny, no SDK.
      - D2TG::Poller + cli/poller (TGT-005): real long-poll loop; inbound
        text messages now print to stdout. No access control yet.
      - D2TG::Store + access-control gate (TGT-006): SQLite allow_list/
        pending tables; D2TG_CHAT_ID auto-seeded, everyone else silenced
        and recorded pending. cpanfile added (DBI, DBD::SQLite).
      - D2TG::Store::approve + cli/approve (TGT-007): d2 tg.approve
        <chat_id> moves a pending sender into the allow-list.
      - D2TG::Store::get_offset/set_offset (TGT-008): poll offset now
        persists across cli/poller restarts.
      - cli/approve (TGT-009): failure message now distinguishes
        already-allowed from never-pending.
      - Pending-sender notification (TGT-010): the poller prints a
        stdout line the first time a new sender is queued pending.
      - Media recognition (TGT-011): photo/document/voice messages now
        announce their type on stdout instead of being silently skipped.
      - D2TG::Config::state_db_path (TGT-012): extracted duplicated
        state-path resolution out of cli/poller and cli/approve.

0.02  2026-09-07
      - D2TG::Telegram send_message/send_voice/split_text_utf16 (TGT-013):
        outbound Bot API support - sendMessage auto-splits at 4000 UTF-16
        units without ever breaking a codepoint; sendVoice uploads via a
        hand-built multipart/form-data body.
      - D2TG::TTS (TGT-013): text -> gtts-cli mp3 -> ffmpeg/libopus ogg,
        fatal on either step's failure (no degraded/partial output).
      - D2TG::Reply + cli/reply (TGT-013): d2 tg.reply <chat_id> <text>
        sends a reply as text + voice note together, never text-only -
        synthesis happens before any Telegram call, so a TTS failure
        sends nothing at all.

0.03  2026-09-07
      - D2TG::Download (TGT-014): downloads a Telegram file_id to a
        local temp file via get_file + file_download_url.
      - D2TG::Transcribe (TGT-014): text <- whisper CLI, refusing any
        *.en (English-only) model checkpoint.
      - D2TG::Poller transcribe_voice wiring + cli/poller (TGT-014): an
        allow-listed sender's voice message is now downloaded and
        transcribed, printed to stdout; a download/transcription
        failure is reported on stderr and does not stop the poller
        loop (non-fatal, unlike D2TG::TTS's outbound fatal rule).
      - Tira monitor-job registration verified (TGT-015, docs only): d2
        tira.job.add --schedule monitor --command "d2 tg.poller" + d2
        tira.job.start feeds the poller's output to that project's
        tira.policy.bridge, proven in a developer-dashboard:latest
        container. Exact commands documented in README.md and
        docs/commands.md. No code changed.

0.04  2026-09-07
      - D2TG::Poller download_media wiring + cli/poller (TGT-016): a
        photo or document message from an allow-listed sender is now
        downloaded (reusing D2TG::Download) and its local path printed;
        a download failure is reported on stderr, non-fatal to the
        poller loop. For photos, the largest PhotoSize (Telegram's last
        array entry) is selected. This closes TGIG-002's original
        inbound-media scope.
      - D2TG::Poller internal refactor (TGT-017): extracted the
        duplicated eval/error-report pattern shared by the voice and
        photo/document branches into one _run_non_fatal helper. Pure
        refactor, no behavior change - existing tests needed zero edits.
      - Test-infrastructure refactor (TGT-018): extracted t/lib/Fake/
        Telegram.pm and t/lib/Fake/Store.pm, the identically-duplicated
        poll-mock shape shared by t/04, t/09, t/17, t/18 (5 -> 1 copy
        each). t/14 and t/15's differently-shaped same-named local
        mocks are untouched (different capabilities). Zero test
        assertions changed.
      - Doc-accuracy pass (TGT-019): verified SKILLS.md/README.md/docs/
        POD against the current code - module list, CLI dispatch names,
        VERSION, and every public sub's POD all cross-checked. Reported
        clean, no drift found.
      - BUGFIX (TGT-021, found via the HOURLY BUG HUNT job, reproduced
        live): D2TG::Store::approve never rolled back on an exception
        mid-transaction, leaving the connection with a dangling open
        transaction - every subsequent approve() call on the same Store
        object (i.e. the poller's whole run) then died with "Already in
        a transaction". Fixed: any exception now triggers rollback
        before re-throwing.

0.05  2026-09-07
      - Reply-command template (TGT-022, per Q-004): every content line
        the poller prints (text, a successfully transcribed voice
        message, a photo/document line) is now followed by a
        REPLY WITH: d2 tg.reply <chat_id> "..." template line - never on
        the pending-notification or error lines. No automatic send;
        this only makes composing the real reply fast.

0.06  2026-09-07
      - aptfile (TGT-025): ffmpeg now installs automatically as part of
        'dashboard skills install tg' on Debian-like hosts. gtts-cli and
        whisper stay manual for now - shipping a requirements.txt was
        found to abort the WHOLE skill install on any PEP-668-enforced
        host (modern Debian/Ubuntu), since dashboard's own
        _install_skill_requirements_txt aborts the rest of the install
        chain on its first failure. That's a gap in Developer Dashboard
        itself, not something this skill can safely work around.
      - Docs (TGT-026): added a Troubleshooting section to
        docs/commands.md for "HTTP request failed (status 401
        Unauthorized)" - confirmed (side-by-side curl getMe + cli/poller
        in a developer-dashboard:latest container) this always means the
        configured D2TG_TOKEN is rejected by Telegram directly, not a
        code bug.

0.07  2026-09-07
      - BUGFIX (TGT-028, live production incident): cli/poller's loop
        had no error handling around D2TG::Poller::run_once - any
        transient getUpdates failure (a real one hit in production: 599
        Internal Exception, a low-level connection failure) propagated
        uncaught and silently killed the whole poller process, which
        then never fetched again until manually restarted. Fixed:
        D2TG::Poller::run_once_safe wraps run_once, logging POLL ERROR
        to stderr and retrying after a short backoff instead of dying.
      - D2TG::Telegram and D2TG::Download now use LWP::UserAgent instead
        of HTTP::Tiny (owner-directed, TGT-028) - cpanfile gained
        LWP::UserAgent as a new dependency.
      - BUGFIX found while verifying TGT-028's own fix: cli/poller never
        set $|=1 (autoflush). Previously this didn't matter because any
        transient failure crashed the process, which flushes all
        filehandles on exit; now that run_once_safe keeps the process
        alive through failures, STDOUT could sit block-buffered
        indefinitely instead of reaching the watched stream in real
        time. Fixed with an explicit $|=1.

0.08  2026-09-07
      - BUGFIX (TGT-027, found via IMPROVEMENT HUNT): cli/reply did not
        validate chat_id was numeric before use, unlike cli/approve.
        A non-numeric first argument silently reached D2TG::Telegram and
        failed there with an opaque error instead of a clean local
        Usage message. Fixed: cli/reply now uses the same /^-?\d+$/
        guard as cli/approve, exiting 2 with a Usage message on STDERR
        for a non-numeric chat_id, before any Telegram call is attempted.

0.09  2026-09-07
      - BUGFIX (TGT-031, live production incident, root-caused via direct
        process inspection): D2TG::Transcribe::_run's system(@cmd) call
        to whisper was fully blocking with no timeout - one slow voice
        transcription blocked the entire poll loop (no new messages of
        any kind fetched) and made the poller unresponsive to Ctrl+C/
        SIGTERM for as long as it ran, since Perl defers signal handling
        until a blocking syscall returns. Fixed: _run now forks and
        polls waitpid every 0.2s instead of blocking on it directly (so
        pending signals get a chance to run), enforces a configurable
        $TIMEOUT (default 300s) that kills the child and reports a
        normal TRANSCRIBE ERROR instead of blocking forever, and
        cli/poller's SIGTERM/SIGINT handlers now call the new
        D2TG::Transcribe::kill_current to end an in-flight transcription
        immediately at shutdown time.

0.10  2026-09-07
      - BUGFIX (TGT-030, live reproduction: Michael sent a voice
        message): whisper's own console output (FP16/CPU warning,
        language-detection lines, raw per-segment transcript lines) was
        inherited straight onto the poller's real stdout, polluting the
        watched NEW TG stream. Fixed: D2TG::Transcribe::_run's forked
        child now reopens its own STDOUT/STDERR onto /dev/null before
        exec - only this project's own structured lines ever reach the
        real stream.

0.11  2026-09-07
      - FEATURE (TGT-029, live usability report): the poller's NEW TG/
        NEW TG VOICE/NEW TG MEDIA lines now carry a "(replying to
        <sender>: <snippet-or-kind>)" suffix when Telegram's
        reply_to_message field is present, naming what a reply targets -
        previously a reply looked identical to a fresh message on
        stdout, giving the monitoring agent no way to tell which prior
        message in a conversation a reply responds to.

0.12  2026-09-07
      - DOCS (TGT-032): added a Troubleshooting entry for "HTTP request
        failed (status 409 Conflict)" - non-fatal (handled by
        run_once_safe/TGT-028), likely caused by a forced (non-clean)
        poller restart, now less likely since TGT-031's prompt-shutdown
        fix.

0.13  2026-09-07
      - BUGFIX (TGT-033, found while investigating TGT-030): D2TG::TTS::
        _run had the identical unredirected system(@cmd) pattern as
        D2TG::Transcribe's (fixed in TGT-030) - gtts-cli/ffmpeg's own
        console output could leak onto d2 tg.reply's stdout/stderr.
        Fixed with the same shape: STDOUT/STDERR are temporarily
        redirected to /dev/null around the system() call and restored
        immediately after.

0.14  2026-09-07
      - REFACTOR (TGT-034, found via IMPROVEMENT HUNT): t/23 and t/25's
        near-identical FD save/redirect/restore/compare boilerplate
        extracted into a shared t/lib/Test/CaptureStdio.pm helper
        (capture_stdio), matching the same test-infra deduplication
        pattern already applied once (TGT-018). No behavior change -
        all existing assertions in both files pass unchanged.

0.15  2026-09-07
      - BUGFIX (TGT-035, live E2E screen recording, 3 problems found and
        fixed): (1) D2TG::Telegram's default LWP::UserAgent had no
        explicit timeout, defaulting to LWP's own 180s - Ctrl+C/SIGTERM
        could still be delayed up to 180s even after TGT-031's
        transcription-timeout fix, since Perl defers signal handling
        until the current blocking get_updates HTTP call returns. Fixed
        with an explicit timeout => 35 (a few seconds above
        get_updates' own 30s server-side hint), genuinely bounding both
        shutdown delay and new-message latency to ~35s. (2)
        D2TG::Poller::_reply_context_suffix's 60-char truncation was far
        too aggressive to be useful context for a real conversation -
        raised to 5000 chars, comfortably exceeding Telegram's own
        4096-char message limit so a quoted message is effectively
        never truncated. (3) A message that appeared to "vanish"
        (no reply-context notification ever appeared for a genuine
        Telegram reply) was very likely caused by the same root cause
        as (1) - the poller's current poll cycle was still blocked and
        had not reached the next cycle to fetch it.

0.16  2026-09-07
      - FEATURE (TGT-036, owner request): the poller now self-refreshes
        when a newer version is installed while it's already running.
        D2TG::Config::skill_version reads the installed VERSION from
        .env; after each poll cycle cli/poller compares it to the
        version it started with, and if it changed, cleanly disconnects
        D2TG::Store's DB handle (new disconnect method) and re-execs
        itself in place (same PID) - a fresh Perl interpreter then loads
        the newly-installed code. A pending shutdown always takes
        priority over a detected version change.

0.17  2026-09-07
      - BUGFIX (TGT-037, live bug report): a large photo/document
        produced an unexplained "MEDIA DOWNLOAD ERROR ... HTTP request
        failed (status 400 Bad Request)" - Telegram's Bot API getFile
        endpoint has a hard, documented 20MB limit. D2TG::Poller now
        checks the message's own declared file_size before attempting a
        download; a file over 20MB is reported with a specific "file
        too large to download" error naming the limit, and the doomed
        getFile call is never attempted at all. Normal-sized files are
        unaffected.

0.18  2026-09-07
      - FEATURE (TGT-038, live design proposal from Michael): a reply to
        an uploaded photo/document previously showed only the bare media
        kind ("document"/"photo") in its reply-context suffix, with no
        way to tell which one. D2TG::Store gains a messages table plus
        record_message/get_message; D2TG::Poller now records a short
        summary of every successfully processed message (its text, its
        transcript for voice, or "<kind> <local_path>" for a downloaded
        photo/document) against that message's own chat_id+message_id.
        _reply_context_suffix looks up the replied-to message's stored
        summary first; only when nothing was ever stored for it (predates
        this feature, or the sender was never allow-listed at the time)
        does it fall back to the original Telegram-payload-only
        behavior, unchanged.

0.19  2026-09-07
      - BUGFIX (TGT-039, code-review bug hunt finding): a voice
        transcript could legitimately contain embedded newlines (whisper
        emits one line per segment), and unlike text messages it was
        printed/stored raw - breaking the single-line-per-event stdout
        contract the whole architecture (Tira monitor job feeder) relies
        on. D2TG::Poller's text-branch sanitization (newline -> literal
        \n, strip other control chars) is now extracted into
        _sanitize_for_stdout and applied to the voice-transcript branch
        too, before both printing and storing (TGT-038's record_message).

0.20  2026-09-07
      - FEATURE (TGT-040, live follow-up question - "where is the message
        id?"): every content line (NEW TG / NEW TG VOICE / NEW TG MEDIA)
        and the REPLY WITH template now name the inbound message's own
        message_id. D2TG::Telegram::send_message/send_voice gain an
        optional reply_to_message_id; D2TG::Reply::send_reply and
        cli/reply (new --reply-to-message-id flag) thread it through, so
        a reply can now be sent as a genuine Telegram-native reply -
        threaded under the original message in Telegram's UI - instead
        of always arriving as a fresh, unthreaded message. Omitting the
        flag is unchanged from before.

0.21  2026-09-07
      - FEATURE (TGT-041, live example - "where is msg80 at msg81 when
        81 is replying to 80"): the reply-context suffix
        ("(replying to <sender>: ...)") now also names the original
        message's own message_id, e.g. "(replying to bob [msg #80]:
        document ...)" - previously only the reply's own message
        (TGT-040) named its id, leaving the original message's id
        invisible unless the operator scrolled back to find its own
        line.

0.22  2026-09-07
      - BUGFIX (TGT-042, hourly bug-hunt finding): cli/reply's
        --reply-to-message-id flag (TGT-040) was recognized anywhere in
        the argument list, so reply text passed as multiple unquoted
        shell words could be silently corrupted if it happened to
        contain the literal token "--reply-to-message-id". The parsing
        logic is now extracted into D2TG::Reply::parse_cli_args and only
        recognizes the flag in the trailing position - the last two
        arguments - matching exactly how the poller's own REPLY WITH
        template always appends it. Trailing usage (the documented/
        template usage) is unaffected.

0.23  2026-09-07
      - DOCS (TGT-043, found via DOC-ACCURACY HUNT): SKILLS.md's
        onboarding-example stdout snippets still showed the pre-TGT-040
        format with no message_id or --reply-to-message-id, so a new
        user following the doc literally would see extra output not
        shown in the example. Updated to match the actual current
        output, matching docs/commands.md's already-accurate
        description.

0.24  2026-09-07
      - BUGFIX (TGT-044, live production incident: "nothing received" -
        no matter text, voice, or attachment): the live poller process
        was found stuck with its Telegram connection wedged in SYN-SENT,
        well past the 35s bound TGT-035 documented - even SIGTERM
        couldn't stop it (Perl only delivers a signal once the blocking
        syscall it's inside of returns), requiring SIGKILL. LWP::
        UserAgent's own timeout evidently does not reliably bound a
        stuck TCP connect() phase. D2TG::Telegram::_call now wraps its
        single blocking request() call in an explicit SIGALRM-based hard
        timeout, which reliably interrupts any blocking syscall
        regardless of which phase it's stuck in.

0.25  2026-09-07
      - FEATURE (TGT-045, live request while diagnosing TGT-044): the
        poller's startup line changes from "d2tg poller starting up
        (token set: yes)" to "d2tg poller starting up (token:
        <first4>...<last4>) (chat_id: <chat_id>)" - useful for
        confirming which bot token and admin chat id actually loaded
        from the environment (relevant exactly when debugging a
        wrong-terminal/stale-env-var situation like TGT-044's incident).
        New D2TG::Config::masked_token helper; chat_id is not a secret
        so it's shown in full.

0.26  2026-09-07
      - FEATURE (TGT-046, live design request): D2TG::Store's messages
        table (TGT-038) gains a read_at column, plus mark_read/is_read.
        Replying via d2 tg.reply --reply-to-message-id <id> now marks
        that message read - but only after both the voice and text
        sends have actually succeeded (D2TG::Reply::send_reply's new
        optional store param), so a failed reply never marks anything
        read. A message with no reply against it stays unread.

0.27  2026-09-07
      - FEATURE (TGT-047, live design request following TGT-046): new
        d2 tg.unread command lists every stored message not yet marked
        read - chat id, message id, sender, timestamp, summary - oldest
        first. Prints "No unread messages." when there are none. New
        D2TG::Store::unread_messages backs it.

0.28  2026-09-07
      - REFACTOR (TGT-050, found via IMPROVEMENT HUNT): D2TG::Telegram's
        DEFAULT_HARD_TIMEOUT constant moved from mid-file to sit with
        the other use statements at the top, matching D2TG::Poller's
        established convention for compile-time declarations. No
        behavior change - full suite unaffected.

0.29  2026-09-07
      - FEATURE (TGT-048, live design request): new d2 tg.history
        command lists stored messages oldest first. Without --since/
        --until, shows the 10 most recent. With either or both given
        (ISO 8601, open-ended on the omitted side), shows only messages
        in that range. New D2TG::Store::recent_messages/
        messages_in_range back it.

0.30  2026-09-07
      - FEATURE (TGT-051, live design request): all five d2 tg.*
        entrypoints (poller, reply, approve, unread, history) accept
        --db <alias> / -d <alias> (or D2TG_DB=<alias> as a fallback),
        naming a Developer Dashboard path alias (see d2 paths) whose
        directory both the SQLite state file AND downloaded attachments
        relocate under - store.sqlite directly, attachments under
        files/. An unknown alias refuses to start with a clear STDERR
        message. Downloaded attachments (photos/documents, not the
        transient voice download) are now content-addressed by their
        own SHA256 hash, so identical content sent any number of times
        only ever occupies one copy of disk space - true deduplication,
        not just a naming convention. New D2TG::Config::resolve_alias_dir/
        attachments_dir/extract_db_flag; D2TG::Download::download_file
        gains an optional dir param.

0.31  2026-09-07
      - FEATURE (TGT-052, live design request following TGT-051): the
        poller now prunes its attachment vault after every poll cycle,
        keeping it at or under a 100MB cap - once exceeded, the oldest
        files (by mtime) are deleted first until back under it. Cheap
        enough to run unconditionally (a directory listing and stat
        calls, no network). New D2TG::Download::prune_vault.

0.32  2026-09-07
      - FEATURE (TGT-049, live design request, design confirmed via
        follow-up Q&A): d2 tg.poller accepts repeatable --chat_id <id>/
        --bot <token> CLI args - each --chat_id starts a new group, each
        --bot attaches to the most recently declared one - polling every
        (chat_id, bot) pair sequentially, round-robin, once per poll
        cycle (one process, no forked child per bot). D2TG_CHAT_ID/
        D2TG_TOKEN fold in as an implicit trailing --chat_id/--bot pair
        through the exact same grouping algorithm, so the plain
        env-var-only case (no CLI args at all) is byte-identical to this
        skill's original single-bot behavior - no separate merge code
        path, no migration step for existing installs. Each bot gets its
        own persisted poll offset (D2TG::Store::get_offset/set_offset
        gain an optional bot key, SHA256-hashed before storage so a live
        token is never persisted in plaintext); D2TG::Store's
        admin_chat_id now also accepts an arrayref to seed every
        declared chat_id allowed. New D2TG::Config::bot_groups.

0.33  2026-09-07
      - BUGFIX (TGT-053, found via manual smoke-testing during TGT-049):
        D2TG::Store's read_at column migration (TGT-046) re-runs on
        every D2TG::Store->new() call and is expected to fail with
        "duplicate column name" on any already-migrated database - that
        expected, already-handled case was still printing a raw DBI
        warning to STDERR every single time (every poller startup or
        restart after the first). PrintError is now suppressed for just
        that one statement; a genuinely unexpected failure still dies as
        before.

0.34  2026-09-07
      - BUGFIX (TGT-054, found via a scheduled bug-hunt pass):
        D2TG::Download::download_file's content-addressed dedup branch
        (TGT-051) never refreshed an existing file's mtime on a dedup
        hit, so D2TG::Download::prune_vault's (TGT-052) oldest-mtime-
        first eviction could delete a frequently re-sent, actively-
        wanted attachment before a truly stale one-time file that merely
        happened to have a newer original download timestamp - the
        opposite of the intended "keep the new, ditch the old" behavior.
        A dedup hit now calls utime() to mark the file as freshly
        referenced, so repeated re-sends correctly keep a file warm
        against pruning.

0.35  2026-09-07
      - HARDENING (TGT-055, found via a scheduled improvement-hunt pass):
        D2TG::Telegram::send_message now validates reply_to_message_id
        is numeric before use, dying with a clear D2TG-level error
        instead of forwarding a bad value into Telegram's API and
        surfacing only as an opaque remote error - matching send_voice's
        existing validation. No functional change for either current
        caller (cli/reply and the poller's own template both already
        validate upstream); this closes a latent asymmetry between the
        two send methods.

0.36  2026-09-07
      - DOC FIX (TGT-056, found via a scheduled doc-accuracy hunt pass):
        README.md/SKILLS.md's Status line was stuck at "v0.33" through
        two shipped releases (v0.34, v0.35) - now correctly names v0.36.
        A new t/50-status-line-version-match.t regression test asserts
        both files' Status line always matches .env's VERSION, so this
        can't silently drift again.

0.37  2026-09-07
      - BUGFIX (TGT-057, found via a scheduled bug-hunt pass):
        d2 tg.reply had no way to select a bot token other than
        D2TG_TOKEN, so a message from a chat served only by a
        TGT-049 multi-bot CLI-declared --bot (D2TG_TOKEN left unset)
        produced a REPLY WITH template that died immediately when run
        ("D2TG::Telegram->new requires a token"). d2 tg.poller's
        REPLY WITH template now names the receiving bot (--bot <token>)
        whenever it's running in multi-bot mode - single-bot/env-only
        mode is unchanged, no --bot printed. d2 tg.reply gains a
        leading --bot <token> flag (alongside the existing --db/-d) to
        act on it; omitting it is unchanged (falls back to D2TG_TOKEN).

0.38  2026-09-07
      - BREAKING BUGFIX (TGT-059, live design gap found by the owner):
        --db/-d/D2TG_DB is now mandatory on every d2 tg.* command,
        matching D2TG_CHAT_ID's existing hard-guard pattern exactly -
        this closes a gap where TGT-051's shipped implementation
        silently fell back to storing state under the skill's own
        install directory when the flag/env var was omitted entirely,
        contradicting the owner's original request that omitting it
        should refuse to start. Any invocation without --db/-d/D2TG_DB
        now refuses (STDERR message, exit 1, points at 'd2 paths') -
        an existing install relying on the old silent-fallback behavior
        must now set D2TG_DB (or pass --db/-d) before upgrading.

0.39  2026-09-07
      - DOC FIX (TGT-060, found via a scheduled improvement-hunt pass):
        D2TG::Config::state_db_path/attachments_dir's own POD still
        described their base_dir-omitted fallback branch as a normal,
        reachable path. Since TGT-059 made resolve_alias_dir always
        either die or return a defined directory, no real d2 tg.*
        invocation can omit base_dir anymore - only direct unit tests of
        these two functions can (t/40-db-alias-resolution.t). POD updated
        to say so; no code/behavior change.

0.40  2026-09-07
      - BUGFIX (TGT-062, live production incident reported by the owner:
        "it is not always works. when send message. the poller no
        showing new messages. i need to kill it and reload"):
        root-caused live - a previous d2 tg.poller process suspended via
        job control (Ctrl-Z, not killed) still held an open connection
        to Telegram, silently competing with a freshly started poller
        for the same bot's single-consumer getUpdates slot. A stopped
        process cannot receive any signal (including TGT-044's own
        SIGALRM hard timeout), so no signal-based fix could ever close
        this gap. d2 tg.poller now acquires an exclusive PID-file lock
        under its resolved --db/-d/D2TG_DB storage location at startup
        and refuses to start a second instance while a live one already
        holds it, naming the exact PID to kill; a stale lock left by an
        unclean death (e.g. kill -9) is correctly reclaimed rather than
        blocking a legitimate restart.

0.41  2026-09-07
      - FEATURE (TGT-061, live design request): every NEW TG/NEW TG
        VOICE/NEW TG MEDIA/NEW TG PENDING stdout line is now prefixed
        with a timestamp, e.g. "[2026-09-07 14:32:10] NEW TG [...]".
        Sourced from Telegram's own message.date field (when a message
        was actually received by Telegram), not local wall-clock time -
        so the printed timestamp reflects when the message really
        arrived, even if this poller processed it a poll cycle or more
        later.

0.42  2026-09-07
      - DOC CLARIFICATION (TGT-063, live question): confirmed and
        documented that message_id is entirely Telegram's own per-bot
        sequence number - switching --db/-d/D2TG_DB (or even which bot
        token is in use) has no effect on it and never resets it. No
        code change; this skill was already reading message_id straight
        from Telegram's own payload everywhere.

0.43  2026-09-07
      - BUGFIX (TGT-064, found via a scheduled bug-hunt pass):
        D2TG::Lock::acquire's original check-then-write sequence was not
        atomic - two processes racing to start against the same fresh
        (no pre-existing lock file) --db location could both pass the
        liveness check before either wrote the file, both proceeding to
        run, exactly the concurrent-poller scenario TGT-062 exists to
        prevent. acquire() now uses O_CREAT|O_EXCL as its primary path -
        a true kernel-atomic file creation - with a small bounded retry
        loop for the narrow window where a competing process's file
        exists but hasn't written its PID yet. Verified with a real
        8-process fork-based race: exactly one winner, every run.

0.44  2026-09-07
      - DOC FIX (TGT-065, found via a scheduled doc-accuracy hunt pass):
        docs/commands.md and D2TG::Poller's own POD both claimed EVERY
        event line gets a TGT-061 timestamp prefix - false for the two
        stderr lines (TRANSCRIBE ERROR, MEDIA DOWNLOAD ERROR), which
        never receive the timestamp. Both now correctly scope the claim
        to the 4 stdout event lines and explicitly note the 2 stderr
        lines are not timestamped. No code change.

0.45  2026-09-07
      - BUGFIX (TGT-066, live production incident reported by the owner:
        repeated "POLL ERROR: ... request timed out after 35s" on every
        poll cycle): D2TG::Telegram::DEFAULT_HARD_TIMEOUT (35s) left only
        a 5s margin over get_updates' own 30s long-poll wait - too tight
        for real network/TLS/latency overhead, so a perfectly legitimate,
        successful long-poll response occasionally exceeded 35s total and
        was mistaken for a genuinely stuck connection (a different failure
        mode from TGT-044's actual-hung-connection scenario). Widened to
        50s (a 15s margin); the real production LWP::UserAgent's own
        timeout and the SIGALRM fallback now derive from the exact same
        constant, so they can never silently drift apart again.

0.46  2026-09-07
      - HARDENING (TGT-067, found via a scheduled improvement-hunt pass):
        D2TG::Telegram::get_updates' default long-poll wait (30s) was a
        bare literal, entirely decoupled from DEFAULT_HARD_TIMEOUT - the
        exact margin relationship TGT-066 just fixed a live incident
        over wasn't encoded anywhere in code, only in prose. It now
        derives from DEFAULT_HARD_TIMEOUT minus a new explicit
        DEFAULT_LONG_POLL_MARGIN constant (50-20=30, unchanged
        behavior), so the margin is a code-level invariant that can't
        silently drift apart again if either constant changes in the
        future.

0.47  2026-09-07
      - BUGFIX (TGT-068, found via a scheduled bug-hunt pass, live-
        reproduced): cli/reply hung forever (confirmed: `timeout 3 perl
        cli/reply --bot` never returned) on a bare trailing --bot with
        no token following it - D2TG::Reply::extract_bot_flag's own
        length-2 guard correctly declines to consume anything in that
        case, but cli/reply's flag-parsing loop kept re-entering the
        same branch with the same unshrunk argument list forever
        instead of making forward progress. The --bot branch now shifts
        a bare trailing --bot directly when there's nothing after it to
        consume, falling through to the existing Usage error like any
        other malformed invocation - no more hang.

0.48  2026-09-08
      - BUGFIX (TGT-069, found via a scheduled bug-hunt pass, live-
        reproduced): D2TG::Config::bot_groups silently stored an unusable
        chat_id when --chat_id had no real value following it - a plain
        bare trailing --chat_id stored undef, and (a subtler variant
        found during the fix) a bare trailing --chat_id with D2TG_TOKEN
        also set stored the literal string "--bot" as the chat_id,
        since the env-token merge appends its own --bot <token> pair
        right after the dangling --chat_id, satisfying a naive
        "something is left in argv" check. Either way this eventually
        reached D2TG::Store's SQL bind as an opaque "DBD::SQLite::db do
        failed: datatype mismatch" several layers away from the actual
        mistake. bot_groups now validates the shifted value is defined
        and isn't itself a recognized flag token, dying immediately with
        a clear "--chat_id requires a value" message instead.

0.49  2026-09-08
      - BUGFIX (TGT-070, found via a scheduled improvement-hunt pass,
        live-reproduced, same class of bug as TGT-069): d2 tg.history's
        --since/--until flags didn't validate their value. A bare
        trailing --since silently ran the query completely unscoped
        (printing a misleadingly confident "No messages found." on exit
        0) instead of erroring on the malformed invocation, and --since
        immediately followed by --until swallowed "--until" itself as
        the since value, silently mis-scoping the query to match
        nothing. Both flags now validate their shifted value is defined
        and isn't itself a recognized flag token, exiting 2 with a clear
        "--since/--until requires a value" message instead.

0.50  2026-09-08
      - BUGFIX (TGT-071, found via a scheduled hourly bug-hunt pass,
        live-reproduced, same class of bug as TGT-068/069/070):
        D2TG::Config::extract_db_flag - the shared --db/-d parser used
        by cli/poller, cli/approve, cli/unread, and cli/history - didn't
        validate its shifted value either, and neither did cli/reply's
        own separate --db extraction. A bare or misplaced --db (e.g.
        "cli/history --db --since 2026-01-01", "cli/reply --db --bot
        TOKEN ...", "cli/history --db --chat_id") silently swallowed the
        following flag's own name as the alias, dropping that flag and
        its argument, then failed with a misleading "Unknown --db/-d
        alias '--since'" instead of naming the real problem. Both
        extract_db_flag and cli/reply's own loop now validate the
        shifted value is defined, non-empty, and not itself flag-like,
        dying/exiting with a clear "--db/-d requires a value" message
        instead. All four extract_db_flag callers now eval-wrap the call
        and report the failure the same way they already do for
        resolve_alias_dir's own die.

0.51  2026-09-08
      - IMPROVEMENT (TGT-072, found via a scheduled improvement-hunt
        pass, filed immediately after TGT-071 shipped the 4th
        independent instance of the same validation pattern): the
        "shift a flag's value and validate it isn't missing/empty/
        flag-like" logic had been hand-rolled at 4 separate call sites
        (TGT-068's cli/reply --db loop, TGT-069's bot_groups --chat_id,
        TGT-070's cli/history --since/--until, TGT-071's
        extract_db_flag --db/-d), two checking a generic "looks like a
        flag" pattern and two hardcoding specific sibling flag names
        instead. Extracted into a new D2TG::Config::shift_flag_value,
        now the single shared implementation all 4 call sites use -
        behavior-preserving (existing error messages/exit codes
        unchanged, full suite green with zero regressions) except that
        bot_groups's --chat_id and cli/history's --since/--until now
        use the same generic flag-like check the other two already
        did, instead of only recognizing their own specific sibling
        flag names. A Codex review caught that the initial generic
        check (any leading dash) would have wrongly rejected a
        negative Telegram group/supergroup chat id (always negative,
        e.g. -1001234567890) passed to --chat_id - a real regression
        despite passing every then-existing test, none of which
        exercised a negative chat id. Narrowed to only reject a dash
        immediately followed by a letter (an actual flag name), with
        new regression tests covering the negative-chat-id case.

0.52  2026-09-08
      - BUGFIX (TGT-073, found via a scheduled hourly bug-hunt pass,
        live-reproduced): d2 tg.reply mojibaked non-ASCII reply text
        (accented characters, CJK, Cyrillic, emoji). @ARGV is always
        raw bytes - Perl never decodes it as UTF-8 on its own - so
        D2TG::Reply::parse_cli_args composed reply text straight from
        undecoded bytes, which JSON::PP::encode_json then treated as
        Latin-1 codepoints and re-encoded as UTF-8, double-encoding
        every multi-byte character (e.g. "héllo" arrived on Telegram as
        "hÃ©llo"). parse_cli_args now decodes every argument as UTF-8
        first, fixing it at the one chokepoint every d2 tg.reply
        invocation passes through.

0.53  2026-09-08
      - BUGFIX (TGT-074, found via a code-review pass immediately
        following the TGT-073 bug-hunt, live-reproduced): bot_groups's
        --bot branch and D2TG::Reply::extract_bot_flag both didn't
        validate their shifted token, even after TGT-072's
        shift_flag_value helper existed for exactly this - bot_groups(
        argv=>['--chat_id','1234','--bot']) silently produced
        {chat_id=>1234, bots=>[undef]}, and extract_bot_flag('--bot',
        '--db','myalias',...) silently returned '--db' as the token.
        Both now route through D2TG::Config::shift_flag_value, dying
        with a clear "--bot requires a value" message instead.

0.54  2026-09-08
      - BUGFIX (TGT-075, found via a scheduled search pass, following
        the TGT-074 pipeline-continuity gate): D2TG::Store's
        unread_messages and messages_in_range both ORDER BY created_at
        alone - a second-resolution column with no secondary
        tiebreaker - while recent_messages, querying the same table,
        already adds ", message_id DESC" for exactly this reason.
        Whenever two or more messages land within the same second (a
        realistic burst), SQLite's tie-break order for equal ORDER BY
        keys is not guaranteed to match chronological order, so d2
        tg.unread/d2 tg.history could silently show a same-second burst
        out of sequence despite promising "oldest first." Both now add
        message_id ascending as the tiebreaker, matching
        recent_messages's own already-correct pattern.

0.55  2026-09-08
      - DOC ACCURACY (TGT-076, found via a scheduled doc-accuracy hunt):
        cli/reply's own POD and docs/commands.md's d2 tg.reply section
        both documented --bot's validation as covering only the bare-
        trailing-no-value case (TGT-068), but TGT-074 additionally
        hardened D2TG::Reply::extract_bot_flag to reject --bot
        immediately followed by another flag (e.g. --bot --db myalias)
        - a real behavior change the CLI-facing docs never picked up,
        even though D2TG::Reply.pm's own POD already documented it
        correctly. Both docs now describe the TGT-074 case, mirroring
        --db's own already-correct documentation of the analogous case.

0.56  2026-09-08
      - IMPROVEMENT (TGT-077, found via a scheduled improvement-hunt):
        the 4-line --db/D2TG_DB mandatory-env-setup boilerplate (TGT-059)
        was duplicated verbatim across 5 test files (t/02-poller.t,
        t/06-approve.t, t/21-reply-chat-id-validation.t,
        t/57-reply-bare-bot-flag-no-hang.t,
        t/58-history-since-until-validation.t). Extracted into a new
        t/lib/Test/MandatoryDb.pm (setup_mandatory_db_env), same
        precedent as t/lib/Fake/{Telegram,Store}.pm (TGT-018) and
        t/lib/Test/CaptureStdio.pm (TGT-034) - pure refactor, zero
        assertion changes, full suite green.

0.57  2026-09-08
      - FEATURE (TGT-079, live user request): new optional D2TG_OWNER
        env var. When set, the poller's printed sender name (the main
        content line and any "(replying to ...)" suffix) shows this
        value instead of the raw Telegram username, but only for
        messages from the D2TG_CHAT_ID chat - every other sender's
        username is shown unchanged, and D2TG_OWNER has no effect on
        access control, which still keys entirely on the numeric chat
        id. New D2TG::Config::owner_name and D2TG::Poller::_display_name.

0.58  2026-09-08
      - BUGFIX (TGT-080, found via a scheduled improvement-hunt,
        live-reproduced): D2TG::Download::download_file's
        content-addressed write went directly to its final hash-derived
        path (open/print/close), with no atomicity guarantee. A process
        killed mid-write left a truncated file whose real content no
        longer matched its own filename's claimed hash - and since the
        dedup check only tests file existence (never re-hashes), that
        corrupted file was silently trusted as already-correctly-
        downloaded forever after. Confirmed via a real forked-process
        kill in developer-dashboard:latest. Fixed with a new
        D2TG::Download::_atomic_write helper: write to a temp file in
        the same directory, then rename onto the final path - rename is
        atomic on POSIX filesystems, so a crash before it runs leaves
        nothing at the final path at all.

0.59  2026-09-08
      - FEATURE (TGT-081, live user requests, folded into one ticket):
        a resolved --db/-d/D2TG_DB base directory now nests its state
        and attachments under a .tira/ subdirectory, renamed:
        <base_dir>/store.sqlite -> <base_dir>/.tira/telegram.messages.db,
        <base_dir>/files -> <base_dir>/.tira/attachments. This is a
        breaking layout change for an existing vault - no automatic
        migration is included. Separately: when none of --db/-d/D2TG_DB
        is given at all, TIRA_HOME (if set) is now used as the base
        directory directly instead of refusing to start - only when no
        alias was given at all; an unknown alias still refuses, and if
        neither an alias nor TIRA_HOME is available, every d2 tg.*
        command still refuses to start with a clear error, exactly as
        TGT-059 originally established.
      - DOC ACCURACY (TGT-082, found via JOB-005's scheduled doc-accuracy
        hunt): D2TG::Poller.pm's KNOWN LIMITATION section cited a stale
        35s timeout figure - DEFAULT_HARD_TIMEOUT was widened to 50 by
        TGT-066. Updated to reference the constant instead of a
        hardcoded value.

0.60  2026-09-08
      - FEATURE (TGT-083, live user request): D2TG::Reply::send_reply's
        order was reversed - text is now sent FIRST, then the voice note
        is synthesized, then sent (previously: synthesize, send voice,
        then send text). This is a deliberate, explicit reversal of this
        project's own prior "never text-only" reply-ordering rule (see
        tg-skill-design.md's "Reply design lessons" section and its
        TGT-083 update, and docs/POLICIES.md). A synthesis or send_voice
        failure after the text has already gone out can no longer prevent
        a text-only outcome (Telegram messages can't be unsent), but
        send_reply still dies/propagates the failure loudly so cli/reply
        never falsely reports success.

0.61  2026-09-08
      - FIX (TGT-084, live user request + live production incident):
        D2TG::Lock::acquire no longer refuses to start when it finds a
        live conflicting PID - "last one wins": it SIGKILLs the existing
        holder, waits in a bounded loop for it to actually die, then
        reclaims the lock. Fixes a real production failure loop where a
        monitor job kept restarting d2 tg.poller while a still-live
        previous instance held the lock, and the old refuse-to-start
        behavior meant every restart attempt just failed again
        ("D2TG::Lock: could not acquire ... - contended for too long").
        Also hardened the pre-existing stale-lock reclaim path to be
        atomic (unlink + retry the kernel-atomic O_CREAT|O_EXCL create,
        instead of a non-atomic overwrite) - TGT-084 makes several live
        processes reaching that path at the same instant a real,
        exercised scenario for the first time.

0.62  2026-09-08
      - SECURITY FIX (TGT-086, found by the scheduled hourly bug hunt):
        D2TG::Poller's multi-bot REPLY WITH template no longer prints the
        receiving bot's full, unmasked token to stdout - it is now masked
        (first 4...last 4 characters, matching TGT-045's own startup-line
        masking). Poller stdout is designed to flow into the target
        project's tira.policy.bridge as monitor-output events on a shared
        board, so the previous behavior broadcast a real bot credential
        on every inbound message in multi-bot mode. The printed --bot
        value in multi-bot mode is consequently no longer directly
        runnable as-is; whoever runs d2 tg.reply for that chat must
        supply the real token themselves.

0.63  2026-09-08
      - FEATURE (TGT-087, live user request, following on from TGT-081's
        own vault nesting): cli/poller's single-instance lock file moved
        from a flat poller.pid directly under the resolved --db/-d/
        D2TG_DB storage location to .tira/telegram.pid, matching how
        TGT-081 already nested the vault's other files. New
        D2TG::Config::lock_path helper mirrors state_db_path/
        attachments_dir's own resolution pattern exactly. This is a
        breaking layout change for an already-running poller's lock file
        location - no migration needed, since a lock file has no
        persistent value across a restart.
